# @corpus current
# compile_fail: the SUBJECT of `read` is the stream, never the world.
#
# THE CLAIM ON THIS FILE CHANGED, and the shape under test did not. It used to
# read "io.read is namespace-method syntax — use io:read", which named the
# PUNCTUATION as the objection and `io:read` as the repair. Both halves are now
# wrong: `io:read("*a")` is refused by exactly the same diagnostic as the dotted
# spelling, because the objection is the SUBJECT. `io` supplies streams; it is
# not one. c0 `law.world.grant` — "io:open(path) cannot become canonical merely
# because io is reachable" — and `law.subject.resolve`'s deny row `file:read(stream)`
# are the two rules, and neither mentions a delimiter.
#
# So a fixture pinned on the old sentence would have gone green while naming a
# repair the compiler refuses, which is the failure this directory exists to
# prevent, one level up. The repair is `stdin:read(…)`, and the diagnostic says
# so — direction picks the stream. `gate/refuse.sh` holds the `write` half of
# the same boundary, including the `admits` probes that keep `lib/io.id` legal.

main: i64 = ()
    text = io.read("*a")
    print(text)
    0
